<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!-- Bus: org.legeos.Session. Path: /org/legeos/Session/Battery. What the battery is doing.

     Read straight from sysfs (`/sys/class/power_supply`), not through upower. upower is another
     daemon to depend on, to start, and to be absent; the kernel already exposes every figure
     below in files this service can read unprivileged, and reading them is the whole
     implementation. Nothing here writes anything, so there is no privilege question at all.

     Read-only by construction: this interface has no Set of any kind. A battery is a fact about
     the machine and the only thing a surface can do with it is show it.

     Implemented in services/lege-sessiond/src/apis/battery.rs. -->
<node>
  <interface name="org.legeos.Battery1">

    <!-- The battery as JSON:
         { present, percent, status, on_mains, time_to_empty_minutes, time_to_full_minutes }.

         `present: false` is a desktop, and is a fact rather than a failure. `status` is the
         kernel's own word — "charging", "discharging", "full", "not charging", "unknown" —
         passed through rather than flattened, because "full" and "not charging" are different
         things to say to a person.

         The two time estimates are `null` unless the kernel has the figures to compute them: a
         battery that reports no current draw cannot be given a time remaining, and inventing one
         is worse than leaving the field empty. When several batteries are present their charges
         are summed, which is what a two-battery laptop's user means by "how much is left".

         `on_mains` comes from the Mains supply and is answered even on a machine with no
         battery, because "plugged in" is still true of a desktop. -->
    <method name="Get">
      <arg name="state_json" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
  </interface>
</node>
